{"step_index":152,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T02:02:48Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T10:02:48+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T10:02:49+08:00\nShowing lines 1-57 of 57\n1: # EV high-spec architecture diagrams\n2: \n3: `index.html` is a self-contained page with the A-platform electrical/electronic and IVI hardware views, the B-platform system view, the high-level software view, and its OTA, Diagnostic, Audio, Widevine DRM, and Display child architectures. Open it directly in a browser. Click **OTA & Update Management** or **OTA Service** in the high-level software view to open the OTA architecture; `?tab=software&view=ota` opens OTA directly; click **Diagnostics & Observability**, **Manufacturing Diagnostics**, or **Dcm** for Diagnostics, or use `?tab=software&view=diag`. The OTA diagram now shows directed component interactions and status return; its path controls highlight QNX, Android, MCU, or feedback routes. Empty layers are omitted. Module rows use an aligned grid with orthogonal routes; matched `SPI` and `PKG` ports represent the same connection at its two endpoints, and gaps at crossings indicate no junction. MCU internals are marked as reference-only because the firmware source is not present in this workspace.\n4: \n5: Edit the Python (including `software_ota.py`, `software_ota_graph.py`, `software_diagnostics.py`, `software_diagnostics_graph.py`, `software_audio.py`, `software_audio_graph.py`, `software_widevine.py`, and `software_widevine_graph.py`, `software_display.py`, and `software_display_graph.py`), HTML, CSS, JavaScript, or TSV sources in this directory, then regenerate the page:\n6: \n7: ```sh\n8: python3 build.py\n9: ```\n10: \n11: The build also refreshes `architecture.svg`, `ivi-hardware.svg`, and `b-platform-system.svg`. Commit the edited sources and regenerated outputs together. `template.html` is a build template; open `index.html` for the finished page.\n12: \n13: The B-platform diagram is a vector redraw of the supplied system overview and enlarged detail images. It preserves the three configuration columns, component part numbers, connector positions, branch topology, load matrix, and state colors. Project identifiers and SoC signal-level labels are intentionally omitted.\n14: \n15: \n16: Click **System Image Banks (A/B)** in the OTA graph or its responsibility list to open `storage-partitions.html`. The partition page is a single static table of partition names, device nodes, A/B states, capacities, QNX filesystems, QNX mount points, and a purpose column (说明), with a return link to the OTA view. Keep both HTML files together when copying the viewer. `storage-8295.json` is the portable diagnostic snapshot used to generate it. The table lists partition nodes only, excluding whole LUs, RAM disks, and in-memory IFS mounts. Truncated device names are matched only when unique; unknown partition capacities remain unknown and are never replaced by filesystem sizes.\n17: \n18: The current snapshot was captured on **2026-09-28 15:19:15 UTC+08:00**, the latest run by its manifest capture time. All 35 observed A/B pairs resolve to slot A; the 2026-09-04 snapshot resolved to B. The 115 partition nodes are unchanged. `/mnt` resolves to `system_a` and `/firmware` to `modem_a`. These are snapshot observations, not a claim that both captures came from the same physical device.\n19: \n20: `storage_partition_descriptions.json` keeps purpose notes separate from live diagnostic data, with evidence references to project configuration, the diagnostic run, and AOSP documentation. A/B counterparts share a purpose by their recorded pair name. Any unresolved purpose renders exactly as `unknown`; Android guest mounts appear only in explanations, while the mount columns show QNX observations.\n21: \n22: To import another diagnostic run, pass its directory containing `parsed/` and `raw/`:\n23: \n24: ```sh\n25: python3 import_storage_snapshot.py /path/to/storage-diagnostic/runs/<run>\n26: python3 build.py\n27: ```\n28: \n29: The virtualization overview now separates VM management, vCPU scheduling, memory isolation, VirtIO devices, shared memory, and event notification. The OTA view includes only relevant platform facilities. Project evidence is in `bsp/apps/qnx_ap/target/hypervisor/gvm/ivi/la/linux-la.config` and `la_dp_enabled_b.config` in the HBEZ source workspace. Doorbell is described as a notification mechanism; no independent deployed Doorbell device is claimed. SoC hardware labels identify controllers and interfaces, not TCP/IP.\n30: \n31: The Diagnostic child view uses the same QNX/AAOS/MCU frame, path filters, clickable components, right-click deselection, responsibilities, and interface register as OTA. Its QNX DTC and RPC/SPI paths are grounded in `cluster_source_code/platforms/dtcagent/` and `platforms/rpcd/`, with staged SLM services and binaries corroborating deployment, and its Android blocks in `android/android/vendor/voyah/system/diagnostic/`. The MCU blocks and internal arrows follow the supplied reference image and are marked reference-only because matching MCU firmware source is absent. Matching DTC ports identify the Android Vehicle HAL property and QNX dtcagent endpoints while explicitly leaving the intermediate mapping unverified. The diagram groups dependent components into aligned chains; the wider MCU frame separates UDS transport, DTC storage, and SPI tasks. The responsive SVG uses its own arrow markers so directions remain visible while OTA is hidden.\n32: \n33: The Audio child view opens from **Audio System** or **Audio Device & DSP I/O**, or directly with `?tab=software&view=audio`. It reuses OTA selection, connected-edge highlighting, path filters, keyboard activation, and right-click deselection. `software_audio.py` contains responsibilities and interface notes; `software_audio_graph.py` draws QNX/AAOS layers and audio/device-control paths. `audio-evidence.md` records source evidence and explicit gaps. AVAS and radio paths are document references; no unverified MCU amplifier-control path or active ECNS algorithm is claimed.\n34: \n35: The Audio HFP view separates Java framework services, native services/libraries, and HAL/backend components. Its HFP protocol branch runs through JNI, the native Bluetooth Stack, and Bluetooth HCI HAL. Its parameter branch runs through AudioManager, AudioSystem, AudioFlinger, and AudioDevice. The libhfp_pal extension is contained inside the Audio HAL implementation boundary and configures PAL RX/TX loops; HCI control and I2S speech use separate controller links.\n36: \n37: The Diagnostic **Interaction paths → README** button opens an embedded, offline guide with three SVG sequence diagrams: external UDS request routing and Dcm/Dem processing, Android/QNX fault reporting over RPC/SPI, and the RAM/NvM/Flash persistence lifecycle. `?tab=software&view=diag&readme=1` opens it directly. Close or press Escape to return without losing the selected component or path. `diagnostic_readme.py` contains the guide and diagram source; no CDN or runtime fetch is required. Confirmed source paths, general standard behavior, and unverified MCU/gateway mappings are explicitly distinguished.\n38: \n39: \n40: In the Audio child view, **Bus / BE / TDM routing** opens a source-grounded route explorer. Select any of the 13 output policy buses to see the PAL device, STREAMRX/DEVICERX/DEVICEPP_RX values, and the audited BE profile. Capture/HFP device pairs, conditional ADSP routing parameters, and MIC/A2B slot maps are listed below. `software_audio_routing.py` renders this view from `audio-routing-data.json`; the snapshot embeds source paths/hashes and is included in the self-contained HTML. Physical bus-to-slot/speaker assignments remain `unknown` where the matching ACDB export or A2B ChannelMap is unavailable. Missing A2B zonal resource profiles are explicitly shown; no backend is inferred from a bus number.\n41: \n42: \n43: The Widevine DRM child view opens from **Widevine DRM** in the AAOS **Native / HAL** layer, or directly with `?tab=software&view=widevine`. A single responsive SVG integrates the software calls and protected-media path. TEE is on the left, with QNX Host and Android Guest side by side on the right. Android explicitly separates Applications, Framework, Native / HAL and Linux kernel. QNX groups its host services and drivers independently, while TEE separates trusted services from the secure-platform entry. Hypervisor spans QNX and Android, followed by full-width Hardware and Display Output bands. Secure buffers sit alongside the hardware that reads and writes them. Blue lines carry control calls, green lines carry media bytes, purple dashed lines carry buffer identities or mappings, and orange lines carry output policy/status. Component selection and path filters apply to the complete architecture.\n44: \n45: `software_widevine.py` records the H56EZ/nurburgring source and log evidence, the playback API contract and the interface register; `software_widevine_graph.py` renders the integrated architecture. `software_widevine_sequence.py` renders the numbered playback sequence in four readable phases, covering conditional provisioning, license exchange, the reused secure-call transport, protected decode and display protection. The phase diagrams explain source-level dependencies, not a single observed trace. The source-default QSEECom branch and binary-available Mink/SMCInvoke branch join at SCM/HAB and QNX QCPE before reaching trusted software. The integrated buffer path distinguishes ordinary encrypted input, protected compressed data and protected decoded frames; CP_B_VIDEO and CP_P_VIDEO identify SMMU contexts, not heap names. WFD/HAB exports and imports buffer references rather than copying video pixels. Crypto HW remains a hardware role because the current evidence does not identify the runtime crypto IP. Protected composition may introduce an additional GPU buffer; L1/secure decoding does not establish active HDCP authentication or link encryption.\n46: \n47: \n48: The Widevine Display Output band compares two alternative four-component paths: SoC DP Output, Serializer, Deserializer and Display Panel. Each row groups the screen-side deserializer and panel inside a dashed Display Module boundary. The HDCP path identifies DP HDCP TX/RX and GMSL HDCP TX/RX roles and applies when required by the OTT service. Components retain selection and connected-edge highlighting; encrypted video links participate in the Media data filter. Module duties describe functions and interfaces, while project observations and pending items appear separately as status notes.\n49: \n50: \n51: Software diagram labels and responsibility text support native text selection and copying. Dragging across module text preserves the selection instead of activating a module or following its link. With text selected, right-click opens the browser menu; otherwise, right-click still clears the selected component. Keyboard component activation remains available.\n52: \n53: The Display child view opens from **Display System** in **Platform Services**, or **Display & GPU Drivers**, and supports `?tab=software&view=display`. It shares component selection, adjacent-edge highlighting, path filters, keyboard activation, text copying, and right-click deselection with OTA. `software_display.py` records responsibilities and source evidence; `software_display_graph.py` renders QNX/AAOS layers, OpenWFD/HAB interactions, HGSL/GSL GPU virtualization, buffer references, event/fence return, and the hardware pixel path. QDI/MDP/MDSS, DSI/DP drivers and HAL, and board libraries are components inside the OpenWFD server. The hardware band separately shows the DSI/DP Controller & PHY; control routes configure the hardware while the pixel path runs from the DPU through the controller and physical link to the panel. Screen Configuration (`graphics.conf`) and OpenWFD Configuration (`qcdisplaycfg.xml`) are separate static inputs, connected only to QNX Screen and OpenWFD Server respectively. Guest acquire and output fences remain local. Board configuration defines panel assignment and physical output; Linux-host Weston, VirtIO-GPU and vhost components are outside this HQX/QNX baseline.\n54: \n55: The Display GPU branch follows the GSL Host/Guest reference naming: Android OpenGL/EGL Subdriver and Vulkan Driver feed GSL Client, HGSL and KHAB; QNX separates UHAB/HAB transport from GSL HAB Server → GSL (`libGSLUser.so`) → KGSL. The current Android 13 source supports HWUI SkiaGL/SkiaVulkan, while RenderEngine uses GLES/SkiaGL. Shared command queues and TCSR/GMU doorbell are HGSL capabilities; HAB ports represent control RPC rather than every GPU submission. `content/gunyah/graphics.md` informs the rendering layers; Gunyah `gh_dbl` and Android 16/Graphite details are not part of this HQX/QNX source baseline.\n56: \n57: The Display Android domain follows Java, Native, HAL and Linux Kernel bands: UI traversal/recording at the top, the GPU rendering stack on the left, and Surface → BLAST → SurfaceFlinger → Composer on the right. Native render clients and API loaders are explicit. Gralloc, HWC, SDM and the DRM adapter occupy the HAL band; HGSL and the WFD frontend share Guest HAB through distinct MM_GFX/MM_DISP channels. Matched GFX/DISP ports keep those host connections readable."}
